From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BananaDS <bananads@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NDS: an in-memory snapshot for run-ahead (LeanSavestate),
 loaded back without a JIT reset

BananaDS's run-ahead (runahead=1-2) saves the state after each frame, runs one or two frames ahead with the same
buttons to show the last one's picture, and loads the state back: every frame. melonDS's savestate as it was did
that badly: a load reset the JIT's whole block cache, it wrote 16 MB of main RAM for a DS's 4, a load made the
timing tables and protection maps again (~6 MB), the cart's code was CRC'd twice, and frames run in between left
state a file never needs back. NDS::LeanSavestate, set only around run-ahead's snapshot (never for a file):
- main RAM: 4 MB on a DS; the GPU3D's vertex and polygon RAM and its render list copied as they are (the polygons'
  vertex pointers too: the snapshot never leaves the process); no cart checksum;
- also kept, as a state file doesn't: the divider's and square root's operands and results, the adaptive slice's
  window, each CPU's IRQ line, halt and idle flags and its code/data region timings, the ARM9's instruction cache and
  its replacement seed, and the Wi-Fi access point's beacon clock and client. Without them a game ran differently
  after the undone frames (a frame later at most: GS:DD from frame 16, 399, 406, ...);
- a load leaves the shared WRAM's mapping, the timing tables and the CP15's PU maps alone when what they are made
  from is unchanged, and drops exactly the JIT blocks compiled since the snapshot (ARMJIT::TrackNewBlocks /
  DropNewBlocks: deleted, not retired, their ranges' masks and code protection put right) instead of resetting the
  cache; writes from the undone frames don't count towards a hot self-modifying granule;
- SPU::DiscardOutput: the undone frames add nothing to the sound output (the mixer runs; its output stage, which
  isn't in the state, is left as it was);
- GPU::CaptureFrames counts the frames that started a display capture (the frontend redraws the 3D frame after a
  load then: a capture would carry the frame ahead's 3D into the game's VRAM).
Tested headless (bananads runahead=1 and 2 against no run-ahead, Golden Sun: Dark Dawn, 900 frames with presses,
interpreter, melonDS's renderer unthreaded): main RAM after every frame, the sound byte for byte and the save the
same; each frame shown is the next frame of the run without. Snapshot 6.7 MB; save 0.14 ms, load ~0.1 ms plus the
3D redraw on a PC.
